  "title": "The Synaptic Gap",
  "emoji": "⚡",
  "color": "#8B5CF6",
  "description": "A fast breakdown of The Synaptic Gap for builders.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Neurons almost never touch each other. A gap of 20 nanometers—a thousand times thinner than hair—separates them.",
      "visual": "🔍"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "When an action potential reaches the axon terminal, it triggers calcium ions to rush inward and release neurotransmitters.",
      "visual": "🧪"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Calcium causes vesicles to fuse with the membrane and spill neurotransmitters into the gap.",
      "visual": "💧"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "The whole process takes less than a millisecond—chemistry moving at electrical speed.",
      "visual": "⏱️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "On the other side, receptor proteins catch the neurotransmitters and open ion channels in response.",
      "visual": "🎣"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "The synapse is where electricity becomes chemistry and then electricity again.",
      "visual": "🔄"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What ion triggers neurotransmitter release at the synapse?",
    "options": [
      "Calcium",
      "Sodium",
      "Potassium"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
